From 43c40b75fd1c290d4d2fa12965e387682fe229b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 01:10:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Epic 7B.3: Location Foundation (ADR-012)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds Location as a first-class Organization aggregate — a physical or
recognized work site, orthogonal to the OrgUnit tree (never a node in it,
never an OrgUnit kind). Location identity (id, code) is immutable and never
reused; a rename changes only the display name; locations are archived, never
hard-deleted.

- Location aggregate + Address value object (no independent lifecycle) +
  validation: code format, ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country code, and IANA time
  zone validated via the runtime's own Intl.DateTimeFormat probe (same
  technique Configuration's general company settings use, reimplemented
  locally since Organization must never import Configuration)
- Schema + migration: tenant-scoped unique id/code (mirrors OrgUnit), a
  status CHECK constraint, address flattened onto the table as plain columns
  (no JSON blob) — verified against real Postgres
- Write/read repositories (Prisma + in-memory), event-buffering write
  transaction, and a UnitOfWork committing location + audit + outbox
  atomically, mirroring the OrgUnit/Assignment platform pattern exactly
- LocationService: create, updateDetails, archive — organization.manage
  gated, unique-code-per-tenant enforced in-transaction
- Architecture fitness rules extended: Location read/write-side boundary,
  client-composition guard, and a new org-unit-must-not-import-location rule
  protecting "Location is orthogonal to OrgUnit, never owned by it"; a
  structure test guards a single Location aggregate/service and confirms
  Location is never declared as an OrgUnit kind
- 22 unit tests + 5 real-Postgres integration tests (atomicity, DB-level
  guards, full lifecycle, tenant isolation)

Out of scope, per ADR-012 and ticket: LegalEntity, Location-Employee
assignment, any change to the Assignment aggregate, OrgUnit-Location
ownership, work schedules, geofencing, and a Location administration UI.
Employee.workLocation remains the legacy, non-authoritative field.

